  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sudan ACJR 2024 (Project)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Even before the ongoing famine crisis, Sudan had 3.7 million displaced people in the country with dire humanitarian needs. The fighting between the Sudan Armed Forces (SAF) and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) on multiple fronts have resulted in massive displacement, civilian deaths and injuries, and loss of livelihoods amid deteriorating economic conditions. The conflict has a substantial impact on the country&#x2019;s food system and hinders people&#x2019;s ability to cope with food shortages. Potentially the world&#x2019;s worst hunger crisis could be observed in Sudan with already 18 million people acutely food insecure. North Darfur has continued to be affected by ongoing fighting, with over 2,778 households reportedly displaced to locations across Dar As Salam. From June to Sept 2024, 135,656 people in North Darfur are expected to be in IPC 5, which means even when using all of their coping strategies, people have almost no food and cannot support their basic needs.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Plan International is an active member of humanitarian coordination mechanisms including Child Protection, Education, WASH, Nutrition and FSL sectors. Plan is co-chair with UNOCHA of the Regional Cash Working Group (WG) and an active member of the Sudan Cash MEB Technical WGs. In North Darfur, Plan currently serves as co-chair of the Emergency Response Committee and coordinates the 3-year Joint Response Project (2024-2026) in Sudan. Plan will partner with FPDO, which is currently a partner with Plan in the 3-year Joint Response Project. Both organizations have active humanitarian response projects in North Darfur and have been participating in inter-agency assessments and in cluster mechanisms. Both partners have offices in North Darfur with existing technical and operational staff. In North Darfur, Plan and ZOA,(with their partners) will implement projects. Plan and ZOA have been coordinating on joint MEAL activities and harmonized cash/voucher values. Other Joint Response partners operate in Central Darfur (CD) and South Darfur (SD).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Plan and FPDO will provide both vouchers and cash (446 HHs / 2676 individuals) with protection mainstreaming in the Dar As Salam locality (in North Darfur). The target includes 840 girls (under 18) and 786 boys (under 18).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Plan and FPDO will provide both vouchers and cash assistance, with protection mainstreaming in the Dar As Salam locality. The vouchers will be provided to 446 HHs (2676 individuals) in 3 rounds of $143 per HH, which is 68% of the food basket used by the Cash Working Group and FS Cluster. Cash was included in this response as assessments and consultations suggested that many households may also require other items to meet their basic food needs (cooking utensils, firewood, etc).and that a full voucher approach may thus force some to sell their food to obtain these NFIs. The remaining 32% of the food basket will be provided in cash by FPDO ($67 per HH x3 for 446 HH). Plan has already identified food suppliers who are able to provide the food basket in a manner more efficient than direct procurement. The distribution will be undertaken three times to cover the needs throughout the peak of the lean season. Strong protection mainstreaming is included during distribution in line with the Protection Cluster&#x2019;s Protection Mainstreaming Toolkit and AAP mechanisms will be set up, all building on the lessons learned from the PCJR. FPDO will specifically lead on the cash element but will also lead on activities such as community mobilisation, selection, registration and verification of beneficiaries, community complaint and feedback mechanisms and protection messaging.  Plan will be responsible for overall management and quality of programme implementation as well as voucher transfers, post-distribution monitoring, coordination, as well as monitoring and reporting in close coordination with FPDO. Support for FPDO will focus on protection, gender and AAP. The specific gathering sites within the locality will be selected based on the evolving situation and in consultation with ZOA and their local partner SOS Sahel, local NGOs including DDRA and local communities.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Plan and its local partner FPDO will provide both vouchers and cash assistance, with protection mainstreaming in the Dar As Salam locality (in North Darfur). The vouchers will be provided to 446 HHs (2676 individuals) in 3 rounds of $143 per HH. Cash is also included in this response as assessments and consultations suggested that many households may also require other items to meet their basic food needs (cooking utensils, firewood, etc).and that a full voucher approach may thus force some to sell their food to obtain these NFIs. The remaining 32% of the food basket will be provided in cash by FPDO ($67 per HH, in 3 rounds ,for 446 HH). Plan has already identified food suppliers who are able to provide the food basket in a manner more efficient than direct procurement. The distribution will be undertaken three times to cover the needs throughout the peak of the lean season. Strong protection mainstreaming is included during distribution in line with the Protection Cluster&#x2019;s Protection Mainstreaming Toolkit and AAP mechanisms will be set up.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SUMMARY:
In response to extreme food shortages and catastrophic hunger conditions in Sudan, this project combines Food Security and Livelihoods (FSL) interventions with protection mainstreaming. The project aims to provide vouchers and cash to increase the availability of food and to meet the most acute lifesaving needs caused by the famine-like conditions. Together with a local partner, the project will be implemented in selected internally-displaced people (IDP) gathering sites in the Dar As Salaam locality in North Darfur. The project runs from 7 July 2024 until 31 December 2024.</narrative>
  </description>
